<LessonLayout title="Holy Matrimony" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">Can human love reflect God's love?</p>


<p>
In the wedding at Cana, something remarkable happened that nobody noticed except the servants and the Mother of Jesus. The wine ran out. In that culture, it was a social catastrophe. Mary simply said: "They have no wine." And Jesus, though His hour had not yet come, turned water into wine (Jn 2:1-11). It was His first miracle. Not a healing, not an exorcism, not a raising of the dead. A wedding feast. Christ chose a wedding to reveal His glory for the first time. Marriage, in the Christian vision, is not merely a contract or a social arrangement. It is a sacrament: the love of a man and a woman becoming a visible sign of Christ's love for His Church. As St. Paul wrote: "Husbands, love your wives, as Christ loved the Church and gave Himself up for her" (Eph 5:25).
</p>


</section>

<section class="content">

